WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.568
- For me success is
about the impact you make

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:05.513
and what, kind of, legacy
that you leave behind.

00:00:05.513 --> 00:00:08.808
Say, how many people used
what you created, what

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:10.525
the benefit is overall.

00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:12.312
More than just financial success, I feel

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.867
it's really is more
about making a difference

00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:16.728
in people's lives and
to the world as a whole.

00:00:16.728 --> 00:00:18.272
I'm managing part in
the collegiate capital,

00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:21.521
which is a venture capital
fund and company pull builder.

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:24.249
We're part VC, part start-up.

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:25.849
And more than just writing checks,

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:27.512
our goal is really to help start-ups

00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:28.663
in every stage of the journey.

00:00:28.663 --> 00:00:30.277
So whether it's technical
support, legal support,

00:00:30.277 --> 00:00:33.579
accounting, international expansion,

00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:35.891
hiring corporate, exedra.

00:00:35.891 --> 00:00:38.571
So actually really adding extra value

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:39.880
beyond just a few meetings

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.723
and a check, which is a problem
I feel with a lot of the

00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:44.764
Venture Capital industry at the moment.

00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:45.990
(motivational music)

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.652
I started my first company at 16.

00:00:47.652 --> 00:00:50.196
Since then I've been doing lots of stuff

00:00:50.196 --> 00:00:52.119
and kind of creating my
own companies, getting work

00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:53.887
experience, working in finance.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.000
While in doing contractor full time

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.897
after graduating straight away.

00:00:57.897 --> 00:01:00.165
And actually joined black
broke and get the experience

00:01:00.165 --> 00:01:02.477
and then run my companies
on the side so I had the

00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:04.815
chance to do some banking and consulting,

00:01:04.815 --> 00:01:07.436
I chose asset management
because it turns out

00:01:07.436 --> 00:01:09.151
this market gives you the
freedom, once you've left

00:01:09.151 --> 00:01:10.607
the office, to actually
do things in the evening.

00:01:10.607 --> 00:01:12.471
At the same time you get
that experience, you get

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:14.542
that credibility as well from
working in a large company.

00:01:14.542 --> 00:01:16.150
You get that training.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.242
A lot of people think, oh,
I'll just be my own boss

00:01:18.242 --> 00:01:21.019
and do it from the start, but
actually you do learn a lot

00:01:21.019 --> 00:01:23.250
working for other people
and especially in a large

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.165
company that's become established.

00:01:26.165 --> 00:01:28.925
It makes you more humble in one element.

00:01:28.925 --> 00:01:32.873
And it also helps you get
an understanding of how

00:01:32.873 --> 00:01:34.255
large companies work.

00:01:34.255 --> 00:01:36.806
Because if you're just
a career entrepreneur,

00:01:36.806 --> 00:01:39.392
and there's nothing wrong
with that, if you're only

00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:42.731
used to, kind of, being the
boss and working for yourself

00:01:42.731 --> 00:01:44.669
and working in small
teams, when it gets to that

00:01:44.669 --> 00:01:47.332
stage where you really
need to scale, I feel that

00:01:47.332 --> 00:01:49.551
a lot of earners do fall
down and you have cases

00:01:49.551 --> 00:01:53.243
where the original founders
of a place by outside CEO's

00:01:53.243 --> 00:01:55.565
and outside chairmans, exedra,

00:01:55.565 --> 00:01:59.149
because they're not ready to
run a company of that size.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:01.429
They don't have the skill
set to take it from a

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:04.527
small group of people to a
much, much larger organization.

00:02:04.527 --> 00:02:07.239
The next five years we're
hoping to really make a

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:09.516
big impact in the
venture capital industry,

00:02:09.516 --> 00:02:12.041
work with really exciting
companies, and help

00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:14.351
grow them to the stage
where they can either

00:02:14.351 --> 00:02:15.359
(background laughter)

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:18.271
exit to trade sell or go
to IPA, that's a dream.

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:21.412
And we're looking to
really help create the next

00:02:21.412 --> 00:02:24.382
game changing, innovative companies in

00:02:24.382 --> 00:02:25.514
technology space.

00:02:25.514 --> 00:02:26.776
Particularly out of Europe.

00:02:26.776 --> 00:02:28.474
That's one thing I'm particularly
passionate about, too.

00:02:28.474 --> 00:02:31.613
Catching up with the whole kind of

00:02:31.613 --> 00:02:34.285
entrepreneurshipsy in the
U.S. and Silicon Valley,

00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:35.728
and that's one thing I'm really hoping to

00:02:35.728 --> 00:02:37.579
see the next big unicorns
coming out of Europe.

00:02:37.579 --> 00:02:40.329
(dramatic music)

